  "body": "npm has an unscoped opencode-cursor-auth (mnsosa's, 1.0.16). Searching \"cursor-auth\" on the Plugins page shows it on its own card beside the community Cursor plugin. Its Install sends the bare name ({\"spec\":\"opencode-cursor-auth\"}, install in plugins.js), which resolve takes for `@magpie-community/opencode-cursor-auth`, the community package without its scope (resolve.go:25-33 and 63-75; lines at f8cd964a). So the community plugin is installed, npm's card still says Install, and the status line says \"opencode-cursor-auth is installed\". npm also has unscoped opencode-kiro-auth, opencode-qoder-auth, opencode-zed-auth and opencode-devin-auth.\n\nresolve does this on purpose for a name someone types (resolve.go:35-43, and the CLI says so when it happens, plugins_cli.go:64-65). A card isn't typed, though: it names npm's package. A GitHub card that installs by npm's name (a tagged repository npm has from that repository, github.go:431-432) sends a bare name too.\n\nThe same cards also fail while magpie can't fetch the community list. market() hands back its cached copy together with the fetch's error (market.go:219-230), and resolve refuses a bare name on that error (resolve.go:80-87), so any unscoped npm card's Install fails with \"couldn't read the community plugin list\" when raw.githubusercontent.com is out of reach, and every time with MAGPIE_PLUGIN_MARKET=off (market.go:202).\n\nSending `npm:<name>` from these cards would keep them npm's either way. It also meets the patch I'm posting on #3685, whose Replace dialog names npm's side npm:<name>: with github:someone/opencode-cursor-auth installed, npm's card asks to replace it with npm:opencode-cursor-auth, then sends the bare name, and the community package is installed beside it instead.\n\nI asked npm's registry and search for these names today, and ran the page with a faked API; the install itself wasn't run against the real npm.\n",
  "closed_by": null,
